---
title: Working without Diorama64
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

Diorama64 sets up, builds, runs, and updates your mod for you. You can do all of that yourself instead, with any text editor and a terminal.

## Setting up a project

You need your own copy of Paper Mario (US) as a ROM in `.z64` format. If your ROM is in another format, such as `.n64` or `.v64`, convert it with [Hack64's ROM byte-order converter](https://hack64.net/tools/swapper.php). To make a ROM from your cartridge, see the [dumping guide](https://dumping.guide/carts/nintendo/n64#methods).

{% tabs %}
{% tabitem label="Windows" %}
  {% steps %}
  1. Install [Git for Windows](https://git-scm.com/download/win), keeping all the installer's default options.
  2. Open a terminal and run `git clone https://knot.starhaven.dev/did:plc:dfsxnyhzxwqpmykryrjtjabz papermario-dx`, then `cd papermario-dx`.
  3. Rename your ROM to `baserom.z64`, and put it in the `ver\us` folder inside the project.
  {% /steps %}
{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="macOS" %}
  {% steps %}
  1. Open Terminal and run `xcode-select --install` to install the Xcode Command Line Tools, which provide `git`. Follow the prompts, then wait for the install to finish.
  2. Run `git clone https://knot.starhaven.dev/did:plc:dfsxnyhzxwqpmykryrjtjabz papermario-dx && cd papermario-dx`.
  3. Rename your ROM to `baserom.z64`, and put it in the `ver/us` folder inside the project.
  {% /steps %}
{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Linux" %}
  {% steps %}
  1. Install `git` and `curl` using your distribution's package manager. For example, on Ubuntu or Debian, run `sudo apt install git curl`.
  2. Run `git clone https://knot.starhaven.dev/did:plc:dfsxnyhzxwqpmykryrjtjabz papermario-dx && cd papermario-dx`.
  3. Rename your ROM to `baserom.z64`, and put it in the `ver/us` folder inside the project.
  {% /steps %}
{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Nix" %}
  {% steps %}
  1. Install [Nix](https://nixos.org/download), using the default (multi-user) installer options.
  2. Rename your ROM to `papermario.us.z64`, then run `nix-store --add-fixed sha256 papermario.us.z64` to add it to the Nix store.
  3. Run `git clone https://knot.starhaven.dev/did:plc:dfsxnyhzxwqpmykryrjtjabz papermario-dx && cd papermario-dx`.
  4. Run `nix --extra-experimental-features "flakes nix-command" develop --profile .nix-profile --accept-flake-config`. This downloads every dependency and copies the ROM into the project, so it can take a while the first time. Run the commands on the rest of this page from the shell it opens.
  {% /steps %}
{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

## Where your files go

Diorama64 shows your mod as a single set of files. On disk, those files come from three layers, each a folder with the same layout:

{% filetree %}
- assets
  - dx Assets DX adds or changes (**do not edit**)
  - us Assets from your copy of the game (**do not edit**)
- src Your mod's code and assets
  - dx
    - config.h Mod settings
{% /filetree %}

When more than one layer has a file at the same path, the build uses the highest one: `src/` first, then `assets/dx/`, then `assets/us/`. To change a file from the game, copy it to the same path in `src/` and edit the copy. For example, to change the messages in `assets/us/msg/0C_Chapter1.msg`, copy it to `src/msg/0C_Chapter1.msg`.

The paths in this manual are relative to the layers. For example, `battle/actor/goomba.c` means the file at that path in whichever layer has it, and when the manual asks you to create a file, create it under `src/`.

To remove something a lower layer provides, create a `.meta` file containing `delete: true` at the same path in `src/`. For a folder, put the `.meta` file inside it. For example, `src/sprite/npc/Goomba/.meta` removes the Goomba sprite.

{% aside type="caution" %}
Only create, change, or delete files in `src/`. Never edit `assets/dx/` or `assets/us/`.
{% /aside %}

## Building

Build from the project folder:

{% tabs %}
{% tabitem label="Windows" %}
```
.\tools\build.bat
```
{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Unix" %}
```sh
./tools/build.sh
```
{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

The first build downloads the compiler toolchain. The output ROM is `ver/us/build/papermario.z64`.

If the build fails in a way that doesn't make sense, such as after you update DX, run `./tools/configure.sh --clean` (`.\tools\configure.bat --clean` on Windows) to delete the files from earlier builds, then build again.

## Running

Load `ver/us/build/papermario.z64` in an emulator. Use [ares](https://ares-emu.net), which is accurate enough to show the problems real hardware would. To play on a console, copy the ROM to a flashcart.

{% aside type="caution" %}
If you don't test with ares or real hardware regularly, your mod might not work for other people.
{% /aside %}

## Editing maps and sprites

Diorama64 opens the right editor when you open a map or sprite. Without it, use [Star Rod](https://github.com/z64a/star-rod), which DX downloads with its toolchain. To open it, run `./tools/star-rod.sh` (`.\tools\star-rod.bat` on Windows), or `star-rod` in a Nix shell.

To create a map:

1. Create a folder for the map in its area's folder, such as `src/world/area/cool_city/house_of_fun/`. To start a new area, create the area's folder too.
1. In the map's folder, create `main.c`:

   ```c
   #include "map.h"
   #include "map.xml.h"

   EntryList Entrances = {
       { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 90.0 },
   };

   EvtScript EVS_BindExitTriggers = {
       Return
       End
   };

   EvtScript EVS_Main = {
       Call(SetSpriteShading, SHADING_NONE)
       EVT_SETUP_CAMERA_DEFAULT(0, 0, 0)
       Set(LVar0, Ref(EVS_BindExitTriggers))
       Exec(EnterWalk)
       Return
       End
   };

   OVL_DEF_MAP() = {
       .main = &EVS_Main,
       .entryList = &Entrances,
       .entryCount = ENTRY_COUNT(Entrances),
   };
   ```

1. In Star Rod's Map Editor, select **Create**. For **Name**, enter the map folder's name, such as `house_of_fun`. Star Rod finds the folder and fills in its **Area**.
1. Make the map, and save. Star Rod saves it as `map.xml` in the map's folder.

## Updating DX

Paper Mario DX publishes releases. Each one is a Git tag that ends in a date, in the form `dx-YYYY-MM-DD`, and a fix for an older release adds a number after the date, as in `dx-YYYY-MM-DD.1`. To update your project, merge a release into your `main` branch. Run these commands from the project folder.

### Adding DX as a remote

The first time you update, add Paper Mario DX as a remote named `papermario-dx`:

```sh
git remote add papermario-dx https://knot.starhaven.dev/did:plc:dfsxnyhzxwqpmykryrjtjabz
```

If one of your remotes already points at Paper Mario DX, such as `origin` because you cloned your project from it, rename that remote instead and point it at the address above:

```sh
git remote rename origin papermario-dx
git remote set-url papermario-dx https://knot.starhaven.dev/did:plc:dfsxnyhzxwqpmykryrjtjabz
```

### See what's new

Fetch the releases and list them, newest first:

```sh
git fetch papermario-dx --tags
git tag --list 'dx-*' --sort=-creatordate
```

Before you merge, read the [changelog](/papermario-dx/changelog) for each release newer than the one you have. It says what you need to change in your own project.

### Merging a release

```sh
git checkout main
git merge dx-YYYY-MM-DD
```

Replace `dx-YYYY-MM-DD` with the release you want. If your project is on an older release, merge the newest release rather than each one in turn. A release includes everything before it.

If git reports merge conflicts, it marks each conflicting section in the file with `<<<<<<<`, `=======`, and `>>>>>>>` lines. Edit each file to keep the code you want and remove the markers, then run `git add` on it. When you've resolved them all, run `git commit` to finish the merge.

Then [build](#building) as usual. The first build after an update downloads the toolchain for that release.

### Merging main instead

If you need a change that isn't in a release yet, you can merge `papermario-dx/main`:

```sh
git fetch papermario-dx
git merge papermario-dx/main
```

`main` can hold unfinished work, and its changes aren't in the changelog's release notes until the next release. Every release and every commit on `main` gets a toolchain. A `main` commit's toolchain is deleted 90 days after the next commit lands. If you've merged a `main` commit whose toolchain has been deleted, your build warns you and keeps using the toolchain you already downloaded. A fresh copy of your project has nothing to fall back on, so its build fails with an error that no commit in your history has a published toolchain. Merge a release to fix it.
